WEBVTT

00:00.418 --> 00:01.900
- [Voiceover] What I
want to do in this video

00:01.900 --> 00:05.190
is get some practice, or
become familiar with what

00:05.190 --> 00:08.182
different angle measures in
radians actually represent.

00:08.182 --> 00:10.095
And to get our familiarity,

00:10.095 --> 00:13.788
we're gonna start with a ray
that starts at the origin,

00:13.788 --> 00:16.067
and moves along, and...

00:16.067 --> 00:19.213
Not moves, and points
along the positive X axis.

00:19.213 --> 00:21.076
We're gonna start with this magenta ray,

00:21.076 --> 00:25.753
and we're gonna rotate
it around the origin

00:25.753 --> 00:29.098
counterclockwise by
different angle measures.

00:29.098 --> 00:31.593
And think about what
quadrant do we fall into

00:31.593 --> 00:35.149
if we start with this and we
were to rotate counterclockwise

00:35.149 --> 00:36.636
by three pi over five radians?

00:36.636 --> 00:37.840
And then, if we start with this,

00:37.840 --> 00:39.281
and we were to rotate counterclockwise

00:39.281 --> 00:41.381
by two pi over seven radians?

00:41.381 --> 00:42.550
Or, if we were to start with this,

00:42.550 --> 00:45.329
and then rotate counterclockwise
by three radians?

00:45.329 --> 00:47.571
We encourage you to pause
the video and think about,

00:47.571 --> 00:49.032
starting with this,

00:49.032 --> 00:52.891
if we were to rotate
counterclockwise by each of these,

00:52.891 --> 00:56.433
what quadrant are we going to end up in?

00:57.128 --> 00:59.422
Assume you've paused the video,

00:59.422 --> 01:00.538
and you've tried it out on your own,

01:00.538 --> 01:03.651
so let's try this first
one, three pi over five.

01:03.651 --> 01:06.700
Three pi over five, so
we're gonna start rotating.

01:07.363 --> 01:09.721
If we go straight up,

01:09.721 --> 01:11.763
if we rotate it, essentially,

01:11.763 --> 01:12.967
if you want to think in degrees,

01:12.967 --> 01:15.483
if you rotate it
counterclockwise 90 degrees,

01:15.483 --> 01:18.100
that is going to get us to pi over two.

01:18.827 --> 01:20.531
That would have been

01:20.562 --> 01:24.833
a counterclockwise rotation
of pi over two radians.

01:25.281 --> 01:27.837
Now is three pi over five
greater or less than that?

01:28.207 --> 01:29.866
Well, three pi over five,

01:30.385 --> 01:32.176
three pi over five

01:32.176 --> 01:33.620
is greater than,

01:33.620 --> 01:35.404
or I guess another way I can say it is,

01:35.404 --> 01:39.192
three pi over six is less
than three pi over five.

01:39.192 --> 01:40.419
You make the denominator smaller,

01:40.419 --> 01:42.671
making the fraction larger.

01:43.332 --> 01:46.069
Three pi over six is the
same thing as pi over two.

01:46.069 --> 01:47.296
So, let me write it this way.

01:47.788 --> 01:51.322
Pi over two is less
than three pi over five.

01:51.860 --> 01:53.374
It's definitely past this.

01:53.374 --> 01:55.017
We're gonna go past this.

01:55.017 --> 01:56.921
Does that get us all the way over here?

01:56.921 --> 01:58.407
If we were to go, essentially,

01:58.407 --> 02:00.624
be pointed in the opposite direction.

02:00.624 --> 02:02.827
Instead of being pointed to
the right, making a full,

02:02.830 --> 02:05.838
I guess you could say 180 degree
counterclockwise rotation,

02:05.838 --> 02:07.892
that would be pi radians.

02:07.892 --> 02:08.657
That would be pi radians.

02:08.657 --> 02:10.353
But this thing is less than pi.

02:10.353 --> 02:12.410
Pi would be five pi over five.

02:12.595 --> 02:15.508
This is less than pi radians.

02:15.508 --> 02:17.459
We are going to sit,

02:17.459 --> 02:19.897
we are going to sit someplace,

02:19.897 --> 02:22.637
someplace, and I'm just estimating it.

02:22.637 --> 02:25.993
We are gonna sit someplace like that.

02:26.270 --> 02:29.680
And so we are going to sit
in the second quadrant.

02:30.280 --> 02:32.093
Let's think about two pi seven.

02:32.550 --> 02:36.045
Two pi over seven, do we
even get past pi over two?

02:36.276 --> 02:41.276
Pi over two here would
be 3.5 pi over seven.

02:42.174 --> 02:43.846
We don't even get to pi over two.

02:43.846 --> 02:44.995
We're gonna end up,

02:44.995 --> 02:46.714
we're gonna end up someplace,

02:46.714 --> 02:48.315
someplace over here.

02:48.315 --> 02:50.428
This thing is, it's greater than zero,

02:50.428 --> 02:52.948
so we're gonna definitely
start moving counterclockwise,

02:52.948 --> 02:54.054
but we're not even gonna get to...

02:54.054 --> 02:56.034
This thing is less than pi over two.

02:56.034 --> 02:58.993
This is gonna throw us
in the first quadrant.

02:59.517 --> 03:01.769
What about three radians?

03:01.769 --> 03:03.278
One way to think about it is,

03:03.278 --> 03:05.865
three is a little bit less than pi.

03:06.403 --> 03:10.264
Right? Three is less than pi

03:10.264 --> 03:12.438
but it's greater than pi over two.

03:12.500 --> 03:13.294
How do we know that?

03:13.294 --> 03:18.294
Well, pi is approximately 3.14159

03:18.517 --> 03:20.728
and it just keeps going on and on forever.

03:20.728 --> 03:22.388
So, three is definitely closer to that

03:22.388 --> 03:24.258
than it is to half of that.

03:24.258 --> 03:27.776
It's going to be between
pi over two, and pi.

03:27.776 --> 03:31.947
It's gonna be, if we start
with this magenta ray,

03:31.947 --> 03:35.752
we rotate counterclockwise
by three radians,

03:35.752 --> 03:37.253
we are gonna get...

03:37.253 --> 03:38.594
Actually, it's probably gonna be,

03:38.594 --> 03:39.756
it's gonna look something,

03:39.756 --> 03:41.254
it's gonna be something like this.

03:41.254 --> 03:43.308
But for the sake of this exercise,

03:43.308 --> 03:45.793
we have gotten ourselves, once again,

03:45.793 --> 03:48.800
into the second quadrant.
